  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["U3"],
      "pins": ["U3.2", "U3.3"],
      "nets": ["VCC", "+5V"],
      "observed": "On the sheet (right-hand tile, column 6) U3 is drawn as an LM1117-5.0 with pin 2 labelled VI connected to the VCC power flag and pin 3 labelled VO connected to the +5V power flag (pin 1 = GND). Thus the regulator's input net (VCC) is assigned to footprint pad 2 and its output net (+5V) to footprint pad 3.",
      "expected": "For the LM1117-5.0 in its standard SOT-223/TO-263 package the pinout is pin 1 = GND/ADJ, pin 2 = VOUT, pin 3 = VIN. The schematic symbol's pin numbers must match that: VI belongs on pin 3 and VO on pin 2, so that the raw supply lands on the VIN pad and the regulated +5V on the VOUT pad.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U3 LM1117-5.0: pin 2 = VI (tied to VCC flag), pin 3 = VO (tied to +5V flag), pin 1 = GND (right tile, column 6)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller" }
      ],
      "impact": "The linear regulator is wired with input and output reversed. The +5V rail that powers U1/U2 and the I2C/ISP/pot connectors is not actually regulated; instead the raw VCC (the fan supply, which must exceed 5V for a 5V regulator to make sense) is forced onto the MCU VCC pins through the reverse-connected device, so the board fails to produce a valid 5V rail and the ATtinies are likely over-voltaged.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing is largely internally consistent (ISP, I2C, pot input, high-side PMOS fan switch, THT/SMD MCU options all map sensibly), but the LM1117-5.0 regulator U3 has its VI/VO pins swapped relative to the device's standard SOT-223/TO-263 pinout (pin 2 = VOUT, pin 3 = VIN), wiring the regulator backwards and breaking the +5V rail."
}

